WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.620 --> 00:00:06.100
Fans had to wait more than 30 years, but the
sequel to 1982's Blade Runner is finally here,

00:00:06.100 --> 00:00:07.299
and it's stunning.

00:00:07.299 --> 00:00:11.570
Directed by Denis Villeneuve, Blade Runner
2049 pulls all the sleek visuals from the

00:00:11.570 --> 00:00:16.309
original and wraps them around a new storyline
set 30 years after the original, following

00:00:16.309 --> 00:00:20.580
new blade runner Officer K, played by Ryan
Gosling, who's tasked with rounding up the

00:00:20.580 --> 00:00:22.950
remaining early-generation replicants.

00:00:22.950 --> 00:00:26.880
But it's harder to hunt replicants than it
is to hunt Easter eggs, so we took the easy

00:00:26.880 --> 00:00:31.350
job and found everything you might've missed
in Blade Runner 2049.

00:00:31.350 --> 00:00:32.699
Pale Fire

00:00:32.699 --> 00:00:37.660
Every time K comes out of a stressful situation,
he has to perform a "Post-Trauma Baseline

00:00:37.660 --> 00:00:38.660
Test."

00:00:38.660 --> 00:00:43.570
It's basically a mashup of poetry and gibberish,
with K repeating words like "cell" and "interlinked"

00:00:43.570 --> 00:00:45.800
in response to various prompts.

00:00:45.800 --> 00:00:49.730
It all seems like nonsense, but the movie
left a subtle Easter egg to help fans decode

00:00:49.730 --> 00:00:52.310
what was going on there — sort of.

00:00:52.310 --> 00:00:56.899
As it turns out, all those lines were pulled
from Vladimir Nabakov's novel Pale Fire.

00:00:56.899 --> 00:00:58.660
Specifically, this refrain:

00:00:58.660 --> 00:01:02.079
"Cells interlinked within cells interlinked/Within
one stem.

00:01:02.079 --> 00:01:06.780
And dreadfully distinct/Against the dark,
a tall white fountain played."

00:01:06.780 --> 00:01:11.710
As for what that all means, it's still anyone's
guess, but Nabakov's Pale Fire also happened

00:01:11.710 --> 00:01:15.540
to be the book Joi picked up in K's apartment.

00:01:15.540 --> 00:01:16.610
Enjoy Coca-Cola

00:01:16.610 --> 00:01:21.510
The Los Angeles we see in the first Blade
Runner is awash in the neon glow of corporate

00:01:21.510 --> 00:01:25.620
advertisements playing hundreds of feet high
on the sides of skyscrapers.

00:01:25.620 --> 00:01:28.460
How else would you corner that elusive flying
car demographic?

00:01:28.460 --> 00:01:32.950
But one of the most prominent ads in the first
film is a display for Coca-Cola.

00:01:32.950 --> 00:01:36.560
Ridley Scott explained that the reason he
used the Coke advertisement was that "even

00:01:36.560 --> 00:01:39.820
in a dystopian world, Coca-Cola is everlasting."

00:01:39.820 --> 00:01:47.620
Well, fast forward 30 even dystopian-er years,
and apparently Coca-Cola really is everlasting.

00:01:47.620 --> 00:01:52.071
Even in a world where the only tree is dead
and where people eat grubs to survive, our

00:01:52.071 --> 00:01:56.770
old friends are still pumping out sky-high
treatises to "Enjoy Coca-Cola."

00:01:56.770 --> 00:02:02.450
By the same token, 2049 also had quick Atari
and Pan Am advertisements, if you caught them.

00:02:02.450 --> 00:02:08.310
Unfortunately, Pan Am went defunct in 1991
and Atari was acquired by Hasbro in 1998,

00:02:08.310 --> 00:02:10.729
so they're definitely not around in 2049.

00:02:10.729 --> 00:02:14.200
At least they get to live on through the power
of imagination!

00:02:14.200 --> 00:02:14.940
Right?

00:02:14.940 --> 00:02:16.260
Maybe?

00:02:16.830 --> 00:02:18.380
Sony's hologram

00:02:18.390 --> 00:02:22.549
Speaking of product placement, who would have
a better reason to advertise their name than

00:02:22.549 --> 00:02:25.450
the international distributor of the whole
shebang?

00:02:25.450 --> 00:02:30.580
When K finally tracks down Deckard in Las
Vegas, he plugs a coin into a jukebox and

00:02:30.580 --> 00:02:33.189
brings a hologram of Frank Sinatra to life.

00:02:33.189 --> 00:02:35.319
And the name right there on the front of the
machine?

00:02:35.319 --> 00:02:36.319
Sony.

00:02:36.319 --> 00:02:37.319
Of course.

00:02:37.319 --> 00:02:43.239
Hey, if Peugeot can make flying cars in this
universe, why can't Sony make holograms?

00:02:43.239 --> 00:02:45.249
Vintage inspiration

00:02:45.249 --> 00:02:50.140
Blade Runner 2049's art direction is phenomenal
from start to finish.

00:02:50.140 --> 00:02:55.880
From bleak, rainy L.A. to the trash heaps
of San Diego to the red-tinged Las Vegas smog,

00:02:55.880 --> 00:03:00.040
2049 is perfectly designed to hammer home
all that the apocalyptic ruin.

00:03:00.040 --> 00:03:02.079
And that's just the outside.

00:03:02.079 --> 00:03:05.549
Inside the casino where Deckard lives, the
set design is even more lavish.

00:03:05.549 --> 00:03:07.819
But it didn't all come from thin air.

00:03:07.819 --> 00:03:11.319
In the room where K first encounters Deckard,
there's a grand piano.

00:03:11.319 --> 00:03:15.709
In at least a few shots, you can even see
the name on it: C. Bechstein.

00:03:15.709 --> 00:03:17.189
That's cool, kind of.

00:03:17.189 --> 00:03:18.859
If you're into pianos.

00:03:18.859 --> 00:03:23.790
But as one Reddit user noticed, the imagery
almost perfectly lines up with a 1920's era

00:03:23.790 --> 00:03:25.569
Bechstein poster.

00:03:25.569 --> 00:03:29.400
Everything from the coloring to the ornate
chandelier hanging over the piano seems to

00:03:29.400 --> 00:03:31.720
be inspired by that image.

00:03:31.720 --> 00:03:36.340
If you noticed that, now's the time to go
tell your dad that music history major finally

00:03:36.340 --> 00:03:38.469
paid off.

00:03:38.469 --> 00:03:39.920
Original origami

00:03:39.920 --> 00:03:44.900
You may remember Gaff as the pointy-mustached
blade runner who's always showing up to ruin

00:03:44.900 --> 00:03:46.799
Deckard's day in the first film.

00:03:46.799 --> 00:03:51.230
Fans were happy to see that Gaff made a quick
appearance in Blade Runner 2049 when K was

00:03:51.230 --> 00:03:53.980
trying to track down information about Deckard.

00:03:53.980 --> 00:03:58.739
With all of 20 seconds of screen time, his
cameo in 2049 doesn't serve to advance the

00:03:58.739 --> 00:04:04.069
plot a whole lot, but it does offer up an
opportunity for an on-the-nose Easter egg.

00:04:04.069 --> 00:04:09.069
Just before K leaves, Gaff lays one of his
signature origami figurines on the table.

00:04:09.069 --> 00:04:11.239
That sure looks like a sheep, no?

00:04:11.239 --> 00:04:15.230
Sort of like the name of the Philip K. Dick
novel that inspired Blade Runner in the first

00:04:15.230 --> 00:04:16.230
place.

00:04:16.230 --> 00:04:20.350
Of course, this little moment also begs the
question: If there aren't any trees left,

00:04:20.350 --> 00:04:23.250
where the heck is Gaff getting all this paper?

00:04:23.250 --> 00:04:24.250
WAS that an engineer?

00:04:24.250 --> 00:04:30.160
In 2012, fans scouring the Prometheus DVD
came across a telling Easter egg — a letter

00:04:30.160 --> 00:04:35.160
dictated by Peter Weyland of the Alien universe
that supposedly references Eldon Tyrell of

00:04:35.160 --> 00:04:36.800
the Blade Runner universe.

00:04:36.800 --> 00:04:41.190
The letter never mentions Tyrell by name,
but in it, Weyland calls the man in question

00:04:41.190 --> 00:04:45.590
his "mentor" and says that he's "like a God
on top of a pyramid overlooking a city of

00:04:45.590 --> 00:04:46.590
angels."

00:04:46.590 --> 00:04:50.610
It goes on to say that this guy's robotics
project "literally blew up in the old man's

00:04:50.610 --> 00:04:51.840
face."

00:04:51.840 --> 00:04:56.430
Of course, in Blade Runner, the Tyrell Corporation
was housed in a pyramid-shaped building in

00:04:56.430 --> 00:05:01.229
Los Angeles — a "city of angels" — and
Tyrell's rogue replicant, Roy Batty, crushed

00:05:01.229 --> 00:05:02.229
his skull.

00:05:02.229 --> 00:05:06.629
So while Weyland may need to tone down his
use of the word "literally," the references

00:05:06.629 --> 00:05:07.629
are all there.

00:05:07.629 --> 00:05:12.720
Then, back in May 2017, fans watching the
first trailer for Blade Runner 2049 noticed

00:05:12.720 --> 00:05:17.221
a figure that looks a lot like an engineer
from Prometheus, launching speculation that

00:05:17.221 --> 00:05:22.139
the Tyrell Corporation — sorry, the Wallace
Corporation — is somehow involved with the

00:05:22.139 --> 00:05:23.139
engineers.

00:05:23.139 --> 00:05:25.000
Is it all true after all?

00:05:25.000 --> 00:05:29.430
Unfortunately, the movie gives no hint whatsoever,
but considering the lengths Ridley Scott is

00:05:29.430 --> 00:05:33.630
apparently willing to go to connect these
universes without ever confirming it, it's

00:05:33.630 --> 00:05:36.849
still reasonable to consider this theory open
to discussion.

00:05:36.849 --> 00:05:40.060
If nothing else...it's a pretty cool Easter
Egg, right?

00:05:40.060 --> 00:05:41.060
Thanks for watching!

00:05:41.060 --> 00:05:43.150
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:05:43.150 --> 00:05:45.039
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

